  <h1>The Cosmic Ratio in Time</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">The familiar split of roughly five percent baryonic matter, twenty-seven percent dark
    matter and sixty-eight percent dark energy is a present-epoch model fit, not an eternal recipe. Its changing
    fractions do not by themselves show that one sector converts into another.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The short answer</p>
    <h2 id="t-answer">Yes, It Is a Snapshot; No, a Swap Is Not Yet a Conversion</h2>
    <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> In standard cosmology the density associated with nonrelativistic baryons
      and cold dark matter falls approximately as the inverse cube of the scale factor, while a cosmological
      constant has constant energy density. Their fractions of the total therefore change with cosmic time. Dark
      energy becomes fractionally dominant late without consuming matter.</p>
    <p><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> TTI associates the three sectors with proposed levels of integration:
      completed baryonic integration, partial dark-matter integration and a dark-energy geometric ground. This
      interpretation raises a stronger question: can regions move between levels, and can the preferred
      direction reverse?</p>
    <p>The two statements must not be conflated. A change in fractional dominance can arise from ordinary cosmic
      dilution. A physical exchange requires source terms, a carrier, conserved stress&ndash;energy, compatibility
      with baryon and radiation constraints, and a redshift-dependent signal that improves on the standard
      non-interacting model.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What the three numbers mean</p>
    <h2 id="t-density-parameters">Fractions of a Modelled Critical Density</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The percentages are not three volumes marked off in space. They are dimensionless
      density parameters inferred jointly from observations within a cosmological model.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>The critical density</h3>
      <p>At any epoch, define \(\rho_{\mathrm c}(t)=3H^2(t)/(8\pi G)\). A component has density parameter
        \(\Omega_i(t)=\rho_i(t)/\rho_{\mathrm c}(t)\). For a spatially flat model the full set, including
        radiation at early times, sums to approximately one.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>The present label</h3>
      <p>A subscript zero, as in \(\Omega_{\mathrm b0}\), means the value inferred for the present epoch.
        Quoting \(5{:}27{:}68\) without that time label can make a current relation look timeless. The exact
        numbers also carry measurement uncertainties and model dependence.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>About 13.8 billion years</h3>
      <p>The age is inferred by integrating the expansion history in the fitted model. It is not a stopwatch
        read independently of cosmology. &ldquo;At approximately 13.8 billion years&rdquo; therefore names our
        present location in the model&rsquo;s history, not a numerological threshold selected in advance.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Dark energy is a category</h3>
      <p>In the baseline model it is a cosmological constant with equation-of-state parameter \(w=-1\). More
        general dynamical fields can have \(w\neq-1\), and modified gravity may mimic part of the inference.
        TTI must compete with those possibilities rather than treating one label as one established substance.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Standard density evolution</p>
    <h2 id="t-scaling">The Fractions Change Even When Nothing Converts</h2>
    <p>For a homogeneous component with pressure \(p=w\rho c^2\) and no exchange with other components, the
      continuity equation gives \(\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}\). The standard idealised scalings are</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \rho_{\mathrm r}\propto a^{-4},\qquad
      \rho_{\mathrm b}\propto a^{-3},\qquad
      \rho_{\mathrm{dm}}\propto a^{-3},\qquad
      \rho_\Lambda=\text{constant}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Radiation dilutes as volume expands and each photon is redshifted. Nonrelativistic matter dilutes mainly
      because the same conserved rest mass occupies a larger physical volume. A cosmological constant does not
      dilute. Thus the denominator \(\rho_{\mathrm c}\) and the component densities evolve differently, and
      so do all the \(\Omega_i\).</p>
    <p>Early enough, radiation dominated the expansion. Later, matter dominated. At still later times, the
      approximately constant dark-energy density became dominant over the falling matter density. These are
      ordinary swaps of <em>fractional dominance</em>. They require no dark-energy particle to turn into a dark
      matter particle and no dark matter to become a baryon.</p>
    <p>Baryons and cold dark matter share the same background \(a^{-3}\) scaling after both are nonrelativistic,
      so their global density ratio remains approximately constant in the baseline model. Their local
      distributions differ because baryons collide, radiate, cool, form stars and undergo feedback, while cold
      dark matter is modelled as effectively collisionless.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">A history, not a fixed pie</p>
    <h2 id="t-eras">Four Eras Clarify the Snapshot</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Radiation era <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>At very small scale factor, the \(a^{-4}\) term was dominant. The popular three-part present-day pie
        omits radiation because its fraction is small now, not because radiation was unimportant. Any timeless
        interpretation of 5:27:68 already fails this early-universe comparison.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Matter era <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>After matter overtook radiation, gravitational wells associated mainly with dark matter supported the
        growth of structure. Baryons later fell into those wells while also experiencing pressure, scattering
        and cooling. The two matter sectors played different physical roles despite similar background dilution.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Accelerating era <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>Once the dark-energy term dominated the expansion budget, accelerated expansion followed in the
        baseline model. This does not mean more dark energy had to be manufactured from matter. A constant
        density can win the fraction simply because matter density keeps falling.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Far-future extrapolation <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>If a cosmological constant remains constant and the model continues, its fractional dominance tends
        toward unity as matter becomes increasingly dilute. This is an extrapolation of a successful model, not
        direct observation of the remote future, and it differs from a cyclic return or reversed integration.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Three meanings of &ldquo;swap&rdquo;</p>
    <h2 id="t-three-swaps">Dominance, Redistribution and Conversion</h2>
    <p><strong>First, global dominance:</strong> one component&rsquo;s \(\Omega_i\) becomes larger than another&rsquo;s
      because the background densities scale differently. Radiation&ndash;matter equality and matter&ndash;dark-energy
      equality are examples. No microscopic identity change is implied.</p>
    <p><strong>Second, local redistribution:</strong> components move differently within the same global budget.
      Gas can cool toward a galactic centre while collisionless dark matter remains extended; feedback can expel
      baryons; a cluster collision can separate hot gas from lensing mass. Spatial dominance changes without
      converting one sector into another.</p>
    <p><strong>Third, physical conversion:</strong> energy or particles leave one sector and enter another. This
      requires an interaction term and observables beyond the separate-fluid model. Calling dark matter
      &ldquo;partial integration&rdquo; and baryons &ldquo;complete integration&rdquo; makes conversion imaginable, but
      does not supply the interaction.</p>
    <p><strong>Fourth, reversal of a preferred sequence:</strong> a completed sector might return to a partial or
      ground state. That is stronger again. It needs reverse rates, entropy production, detailed-balance or
      nonequilibrium conditions and a reason the reversal occurs at one epoch or environment rather than
      everywhere.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <p class="kicker">TTI&rsquo;s interpretive map</p>
      <h2 id="t-integration-levels">Three Sectors as Levels of Integration</h2>
      <p>TTI reads dark energy as the geometric ground, dark matter as partial integration that gravitates
        without electromagnetic completion, and baryonic matter as a completed chain able to support atoms,
        chemistry and luminous structure. That map is developed in
        <a href="/gravity/#t-five-percent">Gravity, and the Five Percent</a>.</p>
      <p>The correspondence is not derived from the percentages. Standard cosmology obtains the fractions by
        fitting a physical model to data. TTI has not yet calculated why its proposed levels should yield those
        values, why baryons and dark matter share matter-like dilution, or why the ground should behave like
        \(w\approx-1\).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">A better TTI question</p>
      <h2 id="t-transition-question">Are Levels States, Substances or Descriptions?</h2>
      <p>If the levels are states of one underlying field, transitions may be possible in principle. If they are
        genuinely distinct substances, a reaction and conserved charges are needed. If they are only descriptive
        categories, talk of conversion may be a category error. TTI must choose before writing an exchange law.</p>
      <p>This choice also controls what &ldquo;reverse&rdquo; means. A field can roll back toward another phase, a
        particle can decay through a permitted channel, and a description can change because an observer uses a
        different coarse-graining. These are not interchangeable physical events.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The minimum exchange mathematics</p>
    <h2 id="t-exchange">Conversion Requires Source Terms That Sum to Zero</h2>
    <p>For interacting homogeneous sectors, write one continuity equation per component:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \dot\rho_i+3H(1+w_i)\rho_i=Q_i,
      \qquad
      \sum_i Q_i=0.
    \]</p>
    <p>A positive \(Q_i\) adds energy density to sector \(i\); a negative one removes it. The sum vanishes so
      the combined stress&ndash;energy is conserved in the background model. This equation does not identify the
      microphysics. It is an accounting shell into which a lawful coupling must be placed.</p>
    <p>A TTI transition model might introduce forward and reverse rates between a ground state \(g\), a partial
      state \(p\) and a completed state \(c\). Schematically,
      \(Q_c=\Gamma_{p\to c}\rho_p-\Gamma_{c\to p}\rho_c\), with corresponding opposite terms in
      \(Q_p\). Similar terms could connect \(g\) and \(p\).</p>
    <p>The rates must have units, depend on declared local variables and remain non-negative as rates. Their
      energy release or absorption must appear in other sectors, perhaps radiation or kinetic energy. A net
      sequence can reverse when the reverse rate exceeds the forward rate; no reversal follows merely because
      the universe has become old.</p>
    <p>Perturbations matter as much as the background. An exchange law changes clustering, velocities and
      gravitational potentials. A model tuned to reproduce \(H(z)\) but unable to match lensing, the cosmic
      microwave background and structure growth is not viable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Conservation is more than one zero sum</p>
    <h2 id="t-conservation">What Any Sector Exchange Must Preserve</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Stress&ndash;energy</h3>
      <p>The covariant total must satisfy \(\nabla_\mu T_{\mathrm{total}}^{\mu\nu}=0\). Background
        \(\sum Q_i=0\) is necessary but not sufficient; momentum transfer and perturbations need a consistent
        four-vector exchange.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Baryon number and charge</h3>
      <p>Creating baryonic matter is not merely relabelling energy. The model must explain baryon-number
        accounting, electric neutrality and the particle products. Known baryon-number violation is tightly
        constrained, and the observed matter excess carries an early-universe explanation debt.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Nuclear and thermal history</h3>
      <p>Changing the baryon density around nucleosynthesis or recombination alters light-element abundances,
        acoustic peaks and photon scattering. Late conversion can inject heat, ionisation or gamma rays. The
        absence of such signals limits both rate and channel.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Entropy</h3>
      <p>A cyclic level model cannot silently reset the thermodynamic ledger. Forward and reverse transitions may
        restore a sector fraction while producing net entropy. The companion paper on
        <a href="/entropy-and-self-renewal/">self-renewal</a> distinguishes organisational return from
        microscopic time reversal.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why a reversal is physically demanding</p>
    <h2 id="t-reversal">A Cosmic Return Is Not the Film Run Backward</h2>
    <p>Suppose partial integration becomes completed integration during structure formation, while completed
      structures later dissolve. That would be a forward-time cycle through different macrostates. Stars burn,
      matter disperses and black holes evolve while entropy is produced. The late state is not the exact
      time-reversal of the early state.</p>
    <p>A genuine microscopic reversal would require all relevant momenta, correlations and outgoing radiation to
      be restored with extraordinary precision. A cosmological contraction, a change in sector dominance or a
      return of an order parameter to an earlier value does not perform that operation. It supplies a new branch
      of history.</p>
    <p>TTI can therefore propose recurrent <em>integration levels</em> without claiming that thermodynamic time
      reverses. Let an order parameter \(\chi\) rise and fall while total entropy increases. The result is
      hysteresis: the same \(\chi\) can occur on assembly and release paths with different environments,
      records and entropy.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \chi(t_2)=\chi(t_1)
      \quad\not\Rightarrow\quad
      \text{microstate}(t_2)=\mathcal T\,\text{microstate}(t_1).
    \]</p>
    <p>This is the cosmological analogue of repairing a self. A familiar organisation can return by a new route.
      The distinction lets TTI discuss reversal without contradicting the ordinary thermodynamic arrow by
      definition.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Competing explanations</p>
    <h2 id="t-alternatives">What Must Be Beaten Before Exchange Is Added</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Baseline ΛCDM</h3>
      <p>Non-interacting cold dark matter, baryons, radiation and a cosmological constant already produce
        time-varying fractions and a successful account of many observations. Its known tensions invite tests,
        but they are not automatic evidence for TTI transitions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Dynamical dark energy</h3>
      <p>A field with evolving equation of state can change the expansion history without converting baryons or
        dark matter. Measurements of \(w(z)\) and growth can compare this family with a cosmological constant
        and with interacting models.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Interacting dark-sector models</h3>
      <p>Cosmology already studies possible energy exchange between dark matter and dark energy. TTI must offer a
        distinctive coupling, not merely adopt the generic \(Q_i\) notation. An existing model with fewer
        assumptions is the relevant competitor.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Modified gravity and selection effects</h3>
      <p>Apparent dark-sector behaviour may reflect modified gravitational dynamics, calibration, population
        selection or astrophysical feedback. A claimed integration signature must survive these alternatives in
        held-out data.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Observational debts</p>
    <h2 id="t-observations">The Ratio Must Become a Redshift-Dependent Prediction</h2>
    <p>The model should first predict \(H(z)\), not merely today&rsquo;s three percentages. Supernova distances,
      baryon acoustic oscillations and cosmic chronometers probe the expansion history. A transition timed only
      after seeing those data is a fit, not a prediction.</p>
    <p>It must predict the cosmic microwave background: acoustic peak positions and heights, lensing and the
      inferred matter content. It must also preserve primordial light-element abundances. These measurements
      anchor the baryon density at widely separated early epochs and sharply constrain hidden conversion.</p>
    <p>At later times, weak lensing, redshift-space distortions, galaxy clustering, cluster abundance and peculiar
      velocities measure structure growth. A coupling can alter both the expansion background and how density
      perturbations grow. Consistency across the two is essential.</p>
    <p>Direct or indirect products matter too. Conversion into ordinary particles could yield photons, neutrinos,
      heat, antimatter or ionisation. Conversion out of baryons could affect masses, stable matter or elemental
      abundances. A model must predict why existing searches did not already see the channel.</p>
    <p>The cleanest TTI target would be a prespecified relation among environment, redshift and a transition rate
      that no standard model expects. It should be tested on held-out surveys. A flexible rate function able to
      mimic any expansion history would weaken, not strengthen, the proposal.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Decisive losses</p>
    <h2 id="t-failure">Explicit Failure Conditions</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Dilution explains the alleged swap</h3>
      <p>If all cited evidence is already predicted by \(a^{-3}\), \(a^{-4}\) and constant-Λ scaling, then
        the claim of physical exchange has no evidence. A changing pie chart alone cannot support it.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>No lawful transition model</h3>
      <p>The conversion hypothesis fails as physics if it cannot define the sectors, write covariant source terms,
        preserve total stress&ndash;energy and identify where baryon number, charge and released energy go.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Independent epochs disagree</h3>
      <p>A proposed rate is rejected if it spoils nucleosynthesis, the microwave background, late-time expansion,
        lensing or structure growth when all are fitted consistently, rather than adjusting a separate rate for
        each observation.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>No predictive gain</h3>
      <p>Even a mathematically consistent exchange model loses if ΛCDM or another conventional account predicts
        held-out data as well with fewer parameters, or if the posterior transition rates remain compatible with
        zero at useful precision.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A staged TTI programme</p>
    <h2 id="t-programme">From Metaphor to Competing Cosmology</h2>
    <p><strong>Stage one:</strong> decide what an integration level is. Specify whether \(g,p,c\) are phases of
      one field, separate fluids or effective descriptions. Define an order parameter and its units without
      referring to the observed percentages.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage two:</strong> derive an action, constitutive relation or microphysical reaction. Obtain
      \(T_i^{\mu\nu}\), the exchange four-vectors and stability conditions. Demonstrate that densities stay
      non-negative and perturbations do not develop unphysical ghosts or runaway gradients.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage three:</strong> compute the full history from radiation domination through the present, fitting
      early- and late-universe data jointly. Freeze the transition form before testing a held-out redshift or
      survey range.</p>
    <p><strong>Stage four:</strong> compare Bayesian evidence and predictive accuracy against ΛCDM, dynamical dark
      energy, interacting-dark-sector models and relevant modified-gravity accounts. The words
      &ldquo;integration&rdquo; and &ldquo;return&rdquo; provide no exemption from model comparison.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Earned, chosen, refused</p>
    <h2 id="t-verdict">What the Present Ratio Can and Cannot Say</h2>
    <p><strong>Earned:</strong> the current 5:27:68 relation is epoch-dependent and model-dependent; radiation,
      matter and dark energy have changed fractional dominance over cosmic history; standard density scaling
      explains those changes without sector conversion. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p><strong>Chosen:</strong> TTI interprets the sectors as levels of integration and asks whether a conserved,
      reversible-in-rate but thermodynamically forward transition network connects them. This is a sharper and
      riskier claim than the original static correspondence. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p><strong>Refused:</strong> the present ratio is not treated as an eternal constant, a coded proof of three
      metaphysical levels or evidence that dark energy is eating matter. Fractional dominance, local
      redistribution, sector conversion and microscopic time reversal remain four different propositions.</p>
    <p class="lede">At approximately 13.8 billion years, the universe gives one richly constrained cross-section
      of its history. TTI earns a dynamics only if it predicts other cross-sections better than ordinary cosmic
      dilution does.</p>
  </div>
</section>

